$m=\rho \pi R^{2} t$
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \text { so } R^{2}=\frac{m}{\rho \pi t} \\\\
& I=\frac{m R^{2}}{4}=\frac{m^{2}}{4 \rho \pi t}
\end{aligned}
$$
So $\frac{I_{1}}{I_{2}}=\frac{\rho_{2} t_{2}}{\rho_{1} t_{1}}=\frac{5}{3} \times \frac{0.5}{1}=\frac{5}{6}$
So $x=5$

